WEBVTT

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:22.814
[narrator] <i>Just 50 years ago,</i>

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.276
<i>we finally ventured to the moon.</i>

00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:45.796
<i>For the very first time,</i>
<i>we looked back at our own planet.</i>

00:00:53.387 --> 00:00:58.684
<i>Since then, the human population</i>
<i>has more than doubled.</i>

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:08.610
<i>This series will celebrate</i>
<i>the natural wonders that remain</i>

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.281
<i>and reveal what we must preserve</i>

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:16.910
<i>to ensure people and nature thrive.</i>

00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:35.762
<i>Forests.</i>

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:42.561
<i>Over half of all the world's trees,</i>
<i>evergreen and deciduous,</i>

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:45.689
<i>stand in these great assemblies.</i>

00:01:47.816 --> 00:01:50.569
[birds chirping]

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:59.995
[narrator] <i>For many of us,</i>
<i>they are places of mystery and darkness.</i>

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:15.218
<i>They are key to our climate,</i>
<i>and home to countless unique species.</i>

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:23.143
<i>In the past, we have destroyed them</i>
<i>without hesitation.</i>

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:30.317
<i>Yet, forests do have</i>
<i>an astonishing ability to recover.</i>

00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:57.052
<i>This is the southern edge</i>
<i>of the boreal forest,</i>

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:03.517
<i>the forest that dominates the far north.</i>

00:03:07.479 --> 00:03:11.900
<i>It stretches eastwards across Russia</i>
<i>for thousands of kilometers,</i>

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:16.697
<i>and now, in the middle of winter,</i>
<i>it's largely silent,</i>

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.868
<i>seemingly empty of animals of any kind.</i>

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:31.962
<i>But this is the home</i>
<i>of an animal so rare it's almost mythical.</i>

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:57.988
<i>A Siberian tiger.</i>

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.953
<i>There are less than 600 of them.</i>

00:04:10.292 --> 00:04:12.753
[caws]

00:04:19.593 --> 00:04:21.595
[tiger growls]

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:27.768
[narrator] <i>These are the most intimate </i>
<i>pictures of them in the wild yet taken.</i>

00:04:39.988 --> 00:04:46.244
<i>A male Siberian tiger patrols a territory</i>
<i>of almost 2,000 square kilometers,</i>

00:04:47.078 --> 00:04:50.957
<i>and it has to do so</i>
<i>if it is to find enough prey</i>

00:04:51.208 --> 00:04:54.961
<i>to keep itself alive</i>
<i>during the long winter.</i>

00:05:04.179 --> 00:05:09.518
<i>This is an impoverished land</i>
<i>where food of any kind is scarce.</i>

00:05:14.356 --> 00:05:15.649
<i>A pine cone.</i>

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:23.073
<i>Pine nuts provide vital energy</i>
<i>during the winter months.</i>

00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:33.542
<i>Wild boar depend on these nuts.</i>

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:41.174
<i>For them, it has to be grab and go,</i>

00:05:42.717 --> 00:05:46.596
<i>for they themselves are food for a tiger.</i>

00:05:53.854 --> 00:05:54.688
[squeals]

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:05.407
[narrator] <i>This game of hide-and-seek</i>
<i>is played out over vast areas of forest,</i>

00:06:05.991 --> 00:06:09.953
<i>as all the animals search</i>
<i>for the life-giving stands of pine.</i>

00:06:19.921 --> 00:06:24.092
<i>Poaching has taken the Siberian tiger</i>
<i>to the brink of extinction,</i>

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:29.681
<i>but, since the 1980s,</i>
<i>their numbers have slowly increased.</i>

00:06:40.859 --> 00:06:43.153
<i>These rare glimpses reveal</i>

00:06:43.445 --> 00:06:50.201
<i>that their future still depends on having</i>
<i>vast areas of forest in which to hunt.</i>

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:03.965
<i>The boreal forest extends</i>
<i>from Russia in the east,</i>

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:06.509
<i>across Europe, to North America.</i>

00:07:08.178 --> 00:07:12.432
<i>It contains 750 billion trees,</i>

00:07:13.224 --> 00:07:16.519
<i>and it stores</i>
<i>over 40 percent of the world's carbon,</i>

00:07:17.354 --> 00:07:21.650
<i>making it a vital element</i>
<i>in the fight against climate change.</i>

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:32.202
<i>During the winter, the days are so short</i>
<i>and the temperature so low,</i>

00:07:32.702 --> 00:07:35.121
<i>that growth is at a standstill.</i>

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:46.341
<i>This far north,</i>
<i>the forest can support very few animals</i>

00:07:46.424 --> 00:07:48.885
<i>compared to the rain forests </i>
<i>of the tropics.</i>

00:07:52.764 --> 00:07:57.185
<i>Many are dependent on the supplement</i>
<i>to be found in its rivers.</i>

00:07:58.937 --> 00:08:00.730
[birds caw]

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:14.452
[narrator] <i>In western Alaska,</i>
<i>both in spring and, as now, in autumn,</i>

00:08:14.536 --> 00:08:16.913
<i>salmon, having left the ocean,</i>

00:08:16.997 --> 00:08:20.625
<i>swim up rivers to reach</i>
<i>their ancestral spawning grounds.</i>

00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.463
<i>Their journey from the sea into the forest</i>
<i>represents</i>

00:08:25.547 --> 00:08:29.676
<i>the greatest transfer of nutrients</i>
<i>from one habitat to another</i>

00:08:30.093 --> 00:08:31.803
<i>anywhere on Earth.</i>

00:08:38.893 --> 00:08:41.521
<i>The fish are crucial seasonal food</i>

00:08:41.688 --> 00:08:44.149
<i>for all the predators</i>
<i>that live in this forest.</i>

00:08:48.028 --> 00:08:52.574
<i>Bald eagles live here the year round</i>
<i>and nest close to the river.</i>

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.495
[piercing chirp]

00:09:12.969 --> 00:09:15.847
[narrator] <i>Young eagles, </i>
<i>still without white heads,</i>

00:09:16.181 --> 00:09:19.601
<i>spend their first years</i>
<i>searching for food in the forest.</i>

00:09:21.352 --> 00:09:23.855
<i>But, like this four-year-old female,</i>

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.400
<i>they're now big enough</i>
<i>to try and claim a place on the river.</i>

00:09:33.281 --> 00:09:37.786
<i>At first, all she can get</i>
<i>are the scraps left by others.</i>

00:09:40.997 --> 00:09:44.667
<i>And she can't get even these</i>
<i>without a fight.</i>

00:09:50.131 --> 00:09:52.133
[eagles chittering]

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:07.857
[narrator] <i>She will have to </i>
<i>look elsewhere.</i>

00:10:13.571 --> 00:10:15.657
<i>The boreal forest may be vast,</i>

00:10:16.032 --> 00:10:20.703
<i>but the places where the salmon spawn</i>
<i>are known to all the predators.</i>

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:31.548
<i>As the rivers begin to freeze over,</i>
<i>competition becomes even more intense.</i>

00:10:43.143 --> 00:10:47.188
<i>This youngster is lucky,</i>
<i>but her success is noticed immediately.</i>

00:10:47.564 --> 00:10:50.024
[chittering]

00:11:22.182 --> 00:11:24.184
[narrator] <i>She does have one advantage.</i>

00:11:27.061 --> 00:11:30.481
<i>Females, even when young,</i>
<i>are larger than the males.</i>

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.735
<i>And here, size matters.</i>

00:12:12.065 --> 00:12:13.900
[cawing]

00:12:16.611 --> 00:12:18.238
[narrator] <i>As winter advances,</i>

00:12:18.696 --> 00:12:24.118
<i>young and old have to meet</i>
<i>the challenge of the intense cold.</i>

00:12:26.037 --> 00:12:29.457
<i>If she can survive,</i>
<i>she will have gained a place</i>

00:12:29.749 --> 00:12:33.878
<i>in one of the greatest</i>
<i>and harshest forests on Earth.</i>

00:12:47.141 --> 00:12:48.851
<i>A little further south</i>

00:12:48.935 --> 00:12:52.522
<i>stand the last </i>
<i>of the great redwood forests.</i>

00:12:56.484 --> 00:13:01.739
<i>Not so long ago, these great trees</i>
<i>grew throughout the Pacific Northwest.</i>

00:13:09.205 --> 00:13:12.250
<i>Now, only five percent of them remain.</i>

00:13:22.135 --> 00:13:24.637
<i>Here, conditions are less harsh.</i>

00:13:25.471 --> 00:13:28.933
<i>Winds bring in warm, humid air</i>
<i>from the Pacific Ocean,</i>

00:13:29.017 --> 00:13:32.520
<i>so the trees are able to grow</i>
<i>the year round.</i>

00:13:35.857 --> 00:13:39.193
<i>Hidden in the vegetation</i>
<i>of the moist forest floor</i>

00:13:39.277 --> 00:13:41.988
<i>is a richly populated underworld.</i>

00:13:48.453 --> 00:13:51.372
<i>A male rough-skinned newt.</i>

00:13:54.584 --> 00:13:58.171
<i>Every spring,</i>
<i>he is driven by a mysterious urge</i>

00:13:58.463 --> 00:14:02.008
<i>to return to the pool</i>
<i>where he hatched as a tadpole.</i>

00:14:06.137 --> 00:14:07.972
<i>And he needs to get there quickly.</i>

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.520
<i>Because there are</i>
<i>lots of others like him...</i>

00:14:19.525 --> 00:14:21.652
<i>with exactly the same idea.</i>

00:14:44.008 --> 00:14:45.968
<i>They're all searching for a female,</i>

00:14:47.261 --> 00:14:52.100
<i>but trekking across hundreds of meters</i>
<i>of old-growth forest is not easy.</i>

00:15:00.316 --> 00:15:02.068
<i>There is strong competition.</i>

00:15:11.786 --> 00:15:15.415
<i>And the female is anxious to get going.</i>

00:15:21.421 --> 00:15:23.423
<i>Once paired, he hangs on,</i>

00:15:23.506 --> 00:15:27.718
<i>for she may take several hours</i>
<i>to discharge all her eggs.</i>

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:37.770
<i>Other males are only too eager</i>
<i>to displace him if they can.</i>

00:15:48.739 --> 00:15:50.658
<i>Competition is so vigorous</i>

00:15:50.741 --> 00:15:53.953
<i>that the females</i>
<i>in the center of these writhing balls</i>

00:15:54.203 --> 00:15:56.873
<i>have been known to drown for lack of air.</i>

00:16:08.217 --> 00:16:11.387
<i>But her first partner maintains his grip.</i>

00:16:13.681 --> 00:16:16.601
<i>Within weeks, their tadpoles will hatch.</i>

00:16:19.562 --> 00:16:24.484
<i>And none too soon, for the forest</i>
<i>is about to change dramatically.</i>

00:16:30.072 --> 00:16:34.118
<i>By the end of the summer,</i>
<i>the sun has baked the forest so intensely</i>

00:16:34.577 --> 00:16:36.996
<i>that they are as dry as tinder.</i>

00:17:00.978 --> 00:17:03.272
<i>Strong winds drive the flames,</i>

00:17:04.857 --> 00:17:08.694
<i>and temperatures rise</i>
<i>to 700 degrees Celsius.</i>

00:18:05.418 --> 00:18:09.672
<i>Its aftermath is seemingly</i>
<i>total destruction.</i>

00:18:23.561 --> 00:18:26.814
<i>The wind,</i>
<i>scouring the newly exposed forest floor,</i>

00:18:27.273 --> 00:18:29.358
<i>kicks up dust devils of ash.</i>

00:18:43.331 --> 00:18:46.667
<i>The ground appears to be devoid of life.</i>

00:19:03.476 --> 00:19:06.187
<i>But the forest is far from dead.</i>

00:19:16.155 --> 00:19:18.074
<i>Within only a few months,</i>

00:19:18.366 --> 00:19:22.286
<i>flowers and tree seedlings</i>
<i>will rise from the soil.</i>

00:19:32.171 --> 00:19:37.802
<i>Many, in fact, would not have germinated</i>
<i>had they not received a baptism of fire.</i>

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:52.942
[birds chirping]

00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:58.989
[narrator] <i>Light streaming down</i>
<i>through the newly opened canopy</i>

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:02.827
<i>provides the energy</i>
<i>for a surge of new growth.</i>

00:20:15.965 --> 00:20:19.218
<i>The older, well-established redwoods</i>
<i>have survived,</i>

00:20:19.552 --> 00:20:22.638
<i>protected</i>
<i>by their thick, fire-resistant bark.</i>

00:20:29.103 --> 00:20:34.775
<i>This natural resilience is essential</i>
<i>to the continued health of these forests.</i>

00:20:47.288 --> 00:20:51.041
<i>Most forests, however,</i>
<i>cannot recover on their own.</i>

00:20:54.462 --> 00:20:57.673
<i>Many are helped to recover by animals.</i>

00:21:02.261 --> 00:21:05.765
<i>The trees and their inhabitants</i>
<i>are interdependent.</i>

00:21:10.394 --> 00:21:13.397
<i>And nowhere</i>
<i>is this dependence more apparent</i>

00:21:13.689 --> 00:21:16.108
<i>than in India's Western Ghats.</i>

00:21:17.526 --> 00:21:19.653
<i>This globally important habitat</i>

00:21:19.862 --> 00:21:23.699
<i>contains a third</i>
<i>of all animal species found in India.</i>

00:21:28.788 --> 00:21:30.790
[animals whoop and chirp]

00:21:35.419 --> 00:21:37.505
[narrator] <i>Lion-tailed macaques.</i>

00:21:43.302 --> 00:21:46.013
<i>They feed on the fruits</i>
<i>of many kinds of tree,</i>

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:49.683
<i>and, in doing so,</i>
<i>they distribute the seeds,</i>

00:21:49.767 --> 00:21:52.269
<i>which will emerge unscathed</i>
<i>with their droppings.</i>

00:21:57.316 --> 00:21:59.985
<i>But few monkeys travel very far.</i>

00:22:01.862 --> 00:22:05.699
<i>If plants are to distribute their seeds</i>
<i>across great distances,</i>

00:22:06.367 --> 00:22:08.744
<i>they need another kind of transport.</i>

00:22:21.924 --> 00:22:22.842
<i>Wings.</i>

00:22:44.572 --> 00:22:48.784
<i>Great hornbills have wings</i>
<i>that are almost two meters across,</i>

00:22:49.118 --> 00:22:53.789
<i>and they enable the birds to travel</i>
<i>long distances in search of food.</i>

00:22:57.293 --> 00:23:01.755
<i>Hornbills feed on the fruit</i>
<i>of 40 or so different kinds of trees,</i>

00:23:02.172 --> 00:23:05.175
<i>and transport</i>
<i>the indigestible seeds they contain</i>

00:23:05.759 --> 00:23:07.553
<i>all over the Western Ghats.</i>

00:23:12.725 --> 00:23:17.396
<i>So, it's to the trees' advantage</i>
<i>to attract the hornbill's attention.</i>

00:23:33.579 --> 00:23:36.290
<i>The figs themselves,</i>
<i>although they taste nice,</i>

00:23:36.498 --> 00:23:38.542
<i>are not, in fact, very nutritious,</i>

00:23:38.876 --> 00:23:41.795
<i>so the hornbills have to eat</i>
<i>great quantities of them.</i>

00:23:47.635 --> 00:23:49.011
<i>It can be tiring work.</i>

00:23:52.306 --> 00:23:54.183
<i>But it's not a free-for-all.</i>

00:23:59.521 --> 00:24:03.734
<i>A single fig tree in fruit</i>
<i>attracts great numbers of hornbills.</i>

00:24:12.201 --> 00:24:14.787
<i>Competition between them is intense.</i>

00:24:32.471 --> 00:24:36.850
<i>This aerial jousting between males</i>
<i>has never been filmed before</i>

00:24:37.434 --> 00:24:40.145
<i>and its purpose is not really understood.</i>

00:24:42.189 --> 00:24:45.526
<i>It may be that the birds</i>
<i>are simply squabbling over food,</i>

00:24:46.443 --> 00:24:48.779
<i>but it seems rather more than that.</i>

00:25:00.833 --> 00:25:02.835
[squawking]

00:25:22.229 --> 00:25:25.983
[narrator] <i>The winner  is able to </i>
<i>demonstrate his strength and skill</i>

00:25:26.275 --> 00:25:27.860
<i>to visiting females.</i>

00:25:37.536 --> 00:25:41.457
<i>The males certainly seem keen</i>
<i>to ingratiate themselves.</i>

00:25:51.175 --> 00:25:54.845
<i>It's particularly important for hornbills</i>
<i>to get the best pairing</i>

00:25:55.179 --> 00:25:59.016
<i>because, once established,</i>
<i>the bond between male and female</i>

00:25:59.266 --> 00:26:01.310
<i>will last a lifetime.</i>

00:26:09.026 --> 00:26:11.653
<i>The forest also benefits</i>
<i>from these squabbles,</i>

00:26:12.237 --> 00:26:14.406
<i>because even a defeated hornbill</i>

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:18.660
<i>will spread seeds over wide areas</i>
<i>as he searches for his next meal.</i>

00:26:25.167 --> 00:26:28.545
<i>The relationship</i>
<i>between trees and animals in a forest</i>

00:26:28.754 --> 00:26:30.756
<i>is not always so harmonious.</i>

00:26:33.801 --> 00:26:37.554
<i>This is Africa's largest forest,</i>
<i>the Miombo,</i>

00:26:38.013 --> 00:26:40.432
<i>named after one of its common trees.</i>

00:26:42.851 --> 00:26:45.521
<i>It stretches for over a thousand miles,</i>

00:26:45.604 --> 00:26:48.982
<i>from Angola in the west</i>
<i>to Mozambique in the east.</i>

00:26:59.284 --> 00:27:00.911
<i>At the height of the dry season,</i>

00:27:01.078 --> 00:27:04.998
<i>the Miombo attracts animals</i>
<i>from all across southern Africa.</i>

00:27:05.249 --> 00:27:06.708
[trumpeting]

00:27:17.719 --> 00:27:22.307
[narrator] <i>Elephants prefer grass </i>
<i>if they can get it. It's very nutritious.</i>

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:27.688
<i>In its absence,</i>

00:27:27.855 --> 00:27:32.109
<i>they browse on the abundant leaves</i>
<i>and branches of the Miombo.</i>

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:34.486
[elephants huffing]

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:42.494
[narrator] <i>But they're not the only </i>
<i>hungry ones here.</i>

00:27:47.749 --> 00:27:49.418
<i>These mopane worms</i>

00:27:49.751 --> 00:27:52.296
<i>are not worms, of course,</i>
<i>but caterpillars.</i>

00:27:56.717 --> 00:27:59.970
<i>They hatch simultaneously in huge numbers.</i>

00:28:05.475 --> 00:28:08.270
<i>And just as the forest</i>
<i>is putting out new leaves,</i>

00:28:08.562 --> 00:28:09.813
<i>they begin their attack.</i>

00:28:24.494 --> 00:28:26.121
<i>They feed so voraciously</i>

00:28:26.705 --> 00:28:31.376
<i>that in just six weeks</i>
<i>they increase their size 40 times.</i>

00:28:36.506 --> 00:28:40.052
<i>By the time they're fully developed,</i>
<i>this million-strong army</i>

00:28:40.302 --> 00:28:43.597
<i>will have stripped the entire forest</i>
<i>of its foliage.</i>

00:28:50.395 --> 00:28:52.731
<i>But the Miombo bounces back.</i>

00:28:55.025 --> 00:28:56.401
<i>With the caterpillars gone,</i>

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:59.947
<i>the trees produce</i>
<i>a second growth of leaves.</i>

00:29:06.453 --> 00:29:09.831
<i>This fresh feast then attracts elephants.</i>

00:29:14.962 --> 00:29:17.381
<i>They are less fussy than the caterpillars.</i>

00:29:19.174 --> 00:29:21.468
<i>They will eat every part of the tree.</i>

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:28.934
<i>And a hungry elephant can munch through</i>
<i>200 kilos of vegetation in a day.</i>

00:29:36.608 --> 00:29:39.736
<i>Yet, even this destruction</i>
<i>has its benefits.</i>

00:29:41.405 --> 00:29:46.493
<i>It shapes the forest in a way that helps</i>
<i>one of Africa's most endangered animals.</i>

00:29:50.372 --> 00:29:51.581
<i>Hunting dogs.</i>

00:29:56.086 --> 00:30:00.132
<i>They're seen most frequently,</i>
<i>and most easily, on grasslands.</i>

00:30:00.757 --> 00:30:04.845
<i>But, in fact, these open forests</i>
<i>are their preferred habitat.</i>

00:30:05.762 --> 00:30:06.596
[grunts]

00:30:11.018 --> 00:30:13.478
[narrator] <i>Browsing elephants </i>
<i>open up a forest,</i>

00:30:13.937 --> 00:30:17.107
<i>and that attracts the animals</i>
<i>on which the dogs prey.</i>

00:30:17.733 --> 00:30:18.942
[barks]

00:30:25.782 --> 00:30:28.577
[dogs yipping]

00:30:59.066 --> 00:31:03.320
[narrator] <i>For the dogs, the Miombo forest</i>
<i>is perfect hunting country.</i>

00:31:07.366 --> 00:31:11.578
<i>And it's also an excellent place</i>
<i>in which to bring up pups.</i>

00:31:22.089 --> 00:31:24.716
<i>The pups greet the adults</i>
<i>returning from a hunt</i>

00:31:24.883 --> 00:31:26.218
<i>with great excitement.</i>

00:31:26.426 --> 00:31:28.303
[squealing]

00:31:33.308 --> 00:31:34.309
[narrator] <i>Food.</i>

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:49.658
<i>The first three months of the pups' lives</i>
<i>are spent sheltering underground.</i>

00:31:50.951 --> 00:31:52.744
[soft growling]

00:31:55.038 --> 00:31:57.332
[narrator] <i>Now, they are confident enough</i>

00:31:57.416 --> 00:31:59.835
<i>to stay out in the open</i>
<i>for much of the time.</i>

00:32:01.878 --> 00:32:05.132
<i>And then, like all puppies, they have fun.</i>

00:32:21.857 --> 00:32:24.359
<i>Playing is important for the youngsters,</i>

00:32:24.443 --> 00:32:28.363
<i>for as they do so,</i>
<i>they establish the social bonds</i>

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:31.908
<i>that they will need when they start</i>
<i>to hunt together as a team.</i>

00:32:39.416 --> 00:32:41.418
-[bird squawks]
-[squealing]

00:32:43.628 --> 00:32:46.923
[narrator] <i>The Miombo has always been </i>
<i>important for hunting dogs,</i>

00:32:47.340 --> 00:32:51.595
<i>both as a place to find prey</i>
<i>and as a refuge.</i>

00:32:56.683 --> 00:32:58.477
<i>Never has it been more crucial</i>

00:32:58.768 --> 00:33:02.647
<i>for the survival</i>
<i>of this endangered species than now.</i>

00:33:07.277 --> 00:33:10.697
<i>But they will only survive</i>
<i>if other creatures are here</i>

00:33:11.198 --> 00:33:13.742
<i>to create the kind of habitat they need.</i>

00:33:14.284 --> 00:33:15.744
[elephant lows]

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.835
[narrator] <i>Madagascar has a forest</i>

00:33:24.044 --> 00:33:27.839
<i>dominated uniquely</i>
<i>by one of the oddest of trees.</i>

00:33:29.716 --> 00:33:30.800
<i>Baobabs.</i>

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:40.644
<i>The island has been isolated</i>
<i>for over 80 million years.</i>

00:33:41.937 --> 00:33:45.357
<i>During that immensity of time,</i>
<i>its animals and plants</i>

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:49.653
<i>have evolved into forms</i>
<i>quite different from any elsewhere.</i>

00:33:54.199 --> 00:33:58.161
<i>This makes them one of</i>
<i>the most precious forests on our planet.</i>

00:34:09.005 --> 00:34:13.301
<i>This is not a monkey,</i>
<i>but a distant relative, a lemur,</i>

00:34:13.677 --> 00:34:16.888
<i>and there are</i>
<i>at least 40 different kinds of lemurs,</i>

00:34:17.305 --> 00:34:20.976
<i>all unique to Madagascar</i>
<i>and all endangered.</i>

00:34:21.059 --> 00:34:21.893
[lemur hoots]

00:34:23.061 --> 00:34:25.397
[narrator] <i>Lemurs are crucial </i>
<i>to the forest.</i>

00:34:26.481 --> 00:34:30.235
<i>Without them,</i>
<i>some species of tree cannot survive.</i>

00:34:36.283 --> 00:34:40.287
<i>The forest even has</i>
<i>its own specialized lemur hunter.</i>

00:34:50.255 --> 00:34:52.299
<i>It's Madagascar's top predator,</i>

00:34:53.508 --> 00:34:56.428
<i>and its presence is cause for alarm.</i>

00:35:01.349 --> 00:35:03.852
<i>It can grow</i>
<i>to a length of one and a half meters,</i>

00:35:04.853 --> 00:35:07.564
<i>and it's very seldom seen in the wild.</i>

00:35:09.774 --> 00:35:10.692
<i>It's a fossa.</i>

00:35:20.076 --> 00:35:22.203
<i>For most of the year, fossas are solitary,</i>

00:35:22.704 --> 00:35:27.125
<i>but in the breeding season, they assemble</i>
<i>in a way that is all their own.</i>

00:35:30.211 --> 00:35:35.216
<i>Each mature female takes up residence</i>
<i>in a special mating tree.</i>

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:53.443
<i>Half a dozen or so male fossas</i>
<i>have gathered in the forest below it,</i>

00:35:56.696 --> 00:35:58.782
<i>but she is clearly occupied.</i>

00:36:02.118 --> 00:36:04.496
<i>So, one of the males leaves his scent.</i>

00:36:07.248 --> 00:36:09.793
<i>A visiting card,</i>
<i>just to show that he called.</i>

00:36:24.015 --> 00:36:28.269
<i>He will just have to wait around</i>
<i>and see whether she takes any notice.</i>

00:36:34.067 --> 00:36:37.153
<i>Her present engagement can last all night.</i>

00:36:42.992 --> 00:36:46.830
<i>With its top predators distracted,</i>
<i>the rest of the forest animals</i>

00:36:46.913 --> 00:36:50.792
<i>can go about their normal nightly business</i>
<i>undisturbed.</i>

00:36:54.045 --> 00:36:58.758
<i>These are immature leaf bugs,</i>
<i>a species found only here in Madagascar.</i>

00:37:01.678 --> 00:37:04.764
<i>Why they have this extraordinary shape,</i>
<i>no one knows.</i>

00:37:09.185 --> 00:37:10.979
<i>They feed on tree sap,</i>

00:37:11.396 --> 00:37:15.191
<i>absorbing the part they need</i>
<i>and excreting the rest as a sweet liquid</i>

00:37:15.442 --> 00:37:19.571
<i>sometimes known, perhaps flatteringly,</i>
<i>as honeydew.</i>

00:37:25.618 --> 00:37:31.708
<i>And honeydew, in turn, provides food</i>
<i>for a particular species of small lemur.</i>

00:37:43.803 --> 00:37:47.390
<i>The gray mouse lemur</i>
<i>is only 15 centimeters long,</i>

00:37:47.474 --> 00:37:50.477
<i>one of the smallest primates in the world.</i>

00:37:55.106 --> 00:37:58.943
<i>It's very active</i>
<i>and needs an energy-packed food.</i>

00:38:01.321 --> 00:38:03.740
<i>And while the sugar drops</i>
<i>are clearly delicious,</i>

00:38:04.199 --> 00:38:05.700
<i>they're not very sustaining.</i>

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:13.833
<i>So, she's not going to let</i>
<i>a single drop go to waste.</i>

00:38:38.775 --> 00:38:42.362
<i>The male fossas below</i>
<i>are still awaiting their turn.</i>

00:38:42.445 --> 00:38:43.696
[animal screeches]

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:47.784
[narrator] <i>So, having eaten </i>
<i>as much as she can,</i>

00:38:48.076 --> 00:38:51.204
<i>she retreats</i>
<i>to the safety of her nest hole</i>

00:38:51.329 --> 00:38:53.748
<i>to sleep during the daylight hours.</i>

00:39:16.187 --> 00:39:19.357
<i>Things are not looking so satisfactory</i>
<i>on the ground</i>

00:39:19.566 --> 00:39:21.359
<i>around the fossas' mating tree.</i>

00:39:23.319 --> 00:39:25.071
[sniffs]

00:39:26.656 --> 00:39:29.784
[narrator] <i>The pair above </i>
<i>are clearly still engaged.</i>

00:39:33.705 --> 00:39:38.418
<i>He will either have to find</i>
<i>another mating tree or be very patient.</i>

00:39:46.259 --> 00:39:48.803
<i>But he may well fail altogether.</i>

00:39:51.514 --> 00:39:56.436
<i>A third of Madagascar's fossas</i>
<i>have disappeared in the last 20 years,</i>

00:39:57.395 --> 00:40:01.983
<i>a result of the continued destruction</i>
<i>of their forests by people.</i>

00:40:06.487 --> 00:40:08.239
<i>Since these pictures were recorded,</i>

00:40:08.781 --> 00:40:11.701
<i>this forest,</i>
<i>and the unique life it once contained,</i>

00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:14.203
<i>have disappeared altogether.</i>

00:40:19.792 --> 00:40:24.297
<i>Only three percent</i>
<i>of Madagascar's dry forest remains.</i>

00:40:31.888 --> 00:40:34.182
<i>Worldwide, we have now destroyed</i>

00:40:34.265 --> 00:40:38.144
<i>over half of the forests</i>
<i>that once flourished on our planet.</i>

00:40:41.648 --> 00:40:44.734
<i>Not only are we losing the animals</i>
<i>that once lived in them,</i>

00:40:45.318 --> 00:40:49.030
<i>we are also changing the climate</i>
<i>of the entire globe.</i>

00:41:02.001 --> 00:41:05.004
<i>But our planet's forests,</i>
<i>if given the chance,</i>

00:41:05.171 --> 00:41:07.966
<i>are almost unbelievably resilient.</i>

00:41:13.012 --> 00:41:16.724
<i>Perhaps the best proof we have</i>
<i>of their ability to recover</i>

00:41:17.016 --> 00:41:21.020
<i>can be seen on the site</i>
<i>of one of our greatest disasters.</i>

00:41:29.404 --> 00:41:30.238
<i>Chernobyl.</i>

00:41:36.411 --> 00:41:42.709
<i>In 1986, one of the four reactors</i>
<i>at the Chernobyl nuclear plant exploded.</i>

00:41:44.544 --> 00:41:49.215
<i>It turned this utopian vision</i>
<i>of the future into a ghost town.</i>

00:41:55.722 --> 00:42:00.101
<i>Over 100,000 people</i>
<i>were immediately evacuated,</i>

00:42:00.184 --> 00:42:01.477
<i>never to return.</i>

00:42:08.735 --> 00:42:14.657
<i>The fallout zone was declared</i>
<i>uninhabitable for the next 20,000 years.</i>

00:42:25.418 --> 00:42:29.756
<i>Yet despite the radiation,</i>
<i>there has been a remarkable recovery.</i>

00:42:56.741 --> 00:42:58.576
<i>Within only a decade,</i>

00:42:59.077 --> 00:43:02.538
<i>vegetation began to germinate</i>
<i>in the ruined city.</i>

00:43:09.128 --> 00:43:14.801
<i>And as the forest reestablished itself,</i>
<i>animals began to appear.</i>

00:43:39.617 --> 00:43:43.788
<i>At first it was thought</i>
<i>that these were rare visitors,</i>

00:43:48.501 --> 00:43:51.546
<i>but it soon became clear</i>
<i>that the former city</i>

00:43:51.754 --> 00:43:56.175
<i>was occupied by an established</i>
<i>and thriving wild community.</i>

00:44:08.604 --> 00:44:10.606
[chirping]

00:44:23.411 --> 00:44:25.496
[narrator] <i>Within only 20 years,</i>

00:44:25.580 --> 00:44:28.416
<i>science has recorded </i>
<i>populations of animals</i>

00:44:28.624 --> 00:44:31.586
<i>similar to that</i>
<i>in the wilder parts of Europe.</i>

00:44:35.715 --> 00:44:40.303
<i>Roe deer are now a common sight,</i>
<i>wandering through the suburbs.</i>

00:44:48.561 --> 00:44:51.522
<i>Herds of the endangered Przewalski's horse</i>

00:44:51.731 --> 00:44:54.567
<i>now roam freely</i>
<i>through the once busy city.</i>

00:44:57.653 --> 00:44:59.405
<i>Most surprising of all perhaps,</i>

00:45:00.031 --> 00:45:03.409
<i>the top predator of these forests</i>
<i>has reappeared.</i>

00:45:06.162 --> 00:45:07.246
<i>Wolves.</i>

00:45:11.375 --> 00:45:13.753
<i>Hunters like these would only return</i>

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:18.007
<i>if their prey and the surrounding forest</i>
<i>is also thriving.</i>

00:45:20.218 --> 00:45:22.136
<i>Now, studies have shown</i>

00:45:22.303 --> 00:45:27.975
<i>that there are seven times more wolves</i>
<i>inside the exclusion zone than outside it.</i>

00:45:36.525 --> 00:45:38.569
<i>No unprotected human being</i>

00:45:38.653 --> 00:45:41.864
<i>can stay here for long</i>
<i>without lethal risk.</i>

00:45:44.492 --> 00:45:46.160
<i>But in driving us out,</i>

00:45:46.744 --> 00:45:50.957
<i>the radiation has created space</i>
<i>for wildlife to return.</i>

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:05.263
<i>The dramatic recolonization of Chernobyl</i>
<i>in the space of only 30 years</i>

00:46:05.596 --> 00:46:09.767
<i>is proof</i>
<i>of forests' extraordinary resilience.</i>

00:46:21.570 --> 00:46:25.074
<i>If we choose to give forests</i>
<i>time and space,</i>

00:46:25.491 --> 00:46:27.535
<i>they could reclothe the earth</i>

00:46:27.868 --> 00:46:31.664
<i>with much of the rich and varied</i>
<i>communities of animals and plants</i>

00:46:32.039 --> 00:46:35.251
<i>of which we have, so recently, robbed it.</i>

00:46:39.422 --> 00:46:46.137
<i>A future with more forests</i>
<i>is key to the resilience of our planet.</i>

00:47:03.988 --> 00:47:06.115
<i>Please visit ourplanet.com</i>

00:47:06.407 --> 00:47:10.745
<i>to discover what we need to do now</i>
<i>to restore our forests.</i>

00:47:13.372 --> 00:47:19.211
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Singing together ♪</i>

00:47:21.881 --> 00:47:27.720
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say it's now or never ♪</i>

00:47:30.639 --> 00:47:34.894
<i>♪ 'Cause it's not too late</i>
<i>If we change our ways ♪</i>

00:47:34.977 --> 00:47:38.606
<i>♪ And connect the dots to our problems ♪</i>

00:47:38.856 --> 00:47:44.695
<i>♪ I can hear the whole world</i>
<i>Say we're in this together ♪</i>

00:47:45.029 --> 00:47:46.739
<i>♪ We're in this together ♪</i>